          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Transition to Playwriting</strong><br>\nAfter establishing himself as a critic, Shaw began writing plays. His first significant success came with \"Widowers' Houses,\" which explored the theme of slum landlordism. He wrote more than 60 plays, addressing issues like education, marriage, religion, government, health care, and class privilege.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Major Works and Themes</strong><br>\nSome of Shaw's most famous works include \"Man and Superman,\" \"Pygmalion,\" and \"Saint Joan.\" His plays were known for their comedic satire and commentary on social conditions, challenging audiences to rethink societal norms and the status quo.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nIn 1925, Shaw was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature, honoring his work which was marked by idealism and humanity, its stimulating satire often being infused with a singular poetic beauty. He accepted the award but refused the money.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
