            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Hollywood and \"Pygmalion\"</strong><br>\nShaw's play \"Pygmalion\" was adapted into a popular film in 1938. His involvement with the film and its success brought his work to a wider audience, solidifying his place in the annals of great English playwrights.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shaw's Later Years</strong><br>\nShaw continued to write well into his nineties, maintaining his wit, controversial views, and prolific output. He remained engaged in politics and social issues, never losing his passion for advocating change and critiquing society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nGeorge Bernard Shaw died on November 2, 1950, at the age of 94. His legacy endures in the richness of his plays, which remain relevant for their sharp social commentary and enduring wit. Shaw's work continues to be studied and performed worldwide, influencing generations long after his passing.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
